<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The camera serves everything over plain HTTP: the device description, JSON-RPC, postview
    JPEGs and the live view stream. Android blocks cleartext by default from API 28 onwards, so
    without this the very first request fails with "Cleartext HTTP traffic not permitted".

    Scoped to the camera's address rather than switching cleartext on for the whole app. The
    camera is its own access point with no uplink, so this permits traffic that cannot leave the
    link — and the rest of the app keeps the default refusal.

    192.168.122.1 is the SoftAP gateway Sony bodies use in Wi-Fi Direct mode, which is the only
    mode this module uses — it always joins the camera's own access point, so the camera is always
    the gateway. A camera joined to a home router instead would get an unpredictable DHCP address,
    but nothing here connects that way.

    Entries are exact hostnames. Android's network security config supports neither wildcards nor
    CIDR, so a range such as 192.168.* cannot be expressed and each address must be listed. If a
    body ever turns up elsewhere, the app says so by name: the repository checks the cleartext
    policy before connecting and reports CLEARTEXT_BLOCKED with the address to add here.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">192.168.122.1</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
